 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6100+) It also records compact production and military interval flows plus exact civic, effective state-religion, and war-plan transitions. (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6101+) Technology-acquisition rows also identify whether each technology came from research, trade, a free choice, a goody hut, a Great Person, espionage, a random event, technology sharing, a team merge, inheritance, Advanced Start, Barbarian research, game setup, debug tools, or an unknown Python/script source. (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6102+) Each log also identifies whether the session is a new or loaded single-player, scenario, network, Hotseat, PBEM, Pitboss, or replay game, including its multiplayer turn options. (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6103+) It also synthesizes one summary for every war between civilization teams, including declaration and peace context, elapsed turns, war-success changes, units destroyed and their total production cost, city-plot battle wins, and cities/population captured. Wars already active when a save is loaded are explicitly partial, while wars still active at victory or autoplay end remain marked ongoing rather than being misreported as peace. A full turn-474 test matched all 115 declarations to exactly 91 ordinary-peace summaries, 4 capitulations, 13 state-change/elimination summaries, and 7 wars still ongoing at victory.
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6105+) Setup context also records the calendar, starting turn/year, and each player's actual assigned color with its primary RGB values, which helps connect the text record to maps and screenshots. When the game is won, one final row per civilization pairs its ordinary score with Civ4's normalized final score. (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6104+) Full interval and victory snapshots also record UTC to milliseconds, total real milliseconds since the log session began, and milliseconds since the previous snapshot. This makes autoplay speed and late-game slowdown directly comparable without external timing or timestamp subtraction; real time includes pauses and user interaction. Anonymous process-memory, system memory-load percentage, available 32-bit address space, and exact foreground/minimized samples can be disabled separately without disabling timing or gameplay records. A separate privacy/detail level defaults to display context (resolution, fullscreen/windowed state, and graphics options), Windows versus Wine/Proton, and pointer width; it can be lowered to hide these or raised to add logical processor count and total RAM.
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6106+) Barbarian activity is also preserved without forcing ordinary civilization economy/diplomacy rows onto the Barbarian player. Interval snapshots record Barbarian cities, animals, unit types/roles, and how many units are in Barbarian, unowned, or civilization-owned territory; concise city rows show their development and defenses. At level 3, exact positions, fog/goody-hut/cargo spawn causes, and Barbarian combat outcomes make pressure on nearby civilizations traceable. In a confirming full run, these rows captured 84 spawns, 182 combats, Barbarian city development, and eventual eradication, while adding only about 350-400 rows to an 18.5 MB record.
-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6107+) **Level-3 text-map previews:** `SASGameRecord` includes separate geography, terrain, feature, and political pictures so humans and LLMs can immediately see landmass shapes, islands, ice barriers, narrow passages, environmental cover, and player positions instead of reconstructing them from coordinates. The terrain picture distinguishes Coast from Ocean and overlays ice: one character cannot show both ice and its underlying water terrain, and blocked access matters more strategically. Geography shows broad land/water and plot shapes plus ice barriers without duplicating Coast terrain. The political picture intentionally remains ownership-only, because physical symbols would either hide owned water or appear inconsistently only outside borders. Normally stable geography and terrain appear once at setup/load, with exact later exceptions preserved by coordinate-bearing map-change rows. The initial feature picture shows how jungle, forest, flood plains, oases, and ice affect settling, movement, health, and yields; feature and political pictures then repeat at each full interval and victory snapshot to show forest/jungle clearing and regrowth, late-game fallout, territorial expansion, conquest, and collapse across the run. Blank water and dotted featureless land keep each feature picture readable without overlaying the geography map. Separate structured rows directly record player pollution, its sources, city health, and global warming; the maps supply the spatial context when terrain or features change. Clean fixed-width rows preserve the generated map's proportions in horizontal, square, or vertical maps; legends map configurable symbols to terrain/feature types and political symbols to player IDs, whose setup rows provide the civilization and displayed player name. XML defines control the picture bounds, monospace-width correction, and validated symbols.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6108+) **Level-3 text-map previews:** `SASGameRecord` includes separate geography, terrain, bonus, feature, and political pictures so humans and LLMs can immediately see landmass shapes, islands, ice barriers, narrow passages, resource distribution, environmental cover, and player positions instead of reconstructing them from coordinates. The terrain picture distinguishes Coast from Ocean and overlays ice: one character cannot show both ice and its underlying water terrain, but blocked naval access matters more strategically and the obscured Coast shape is normally inferable from adjacent Coast and land. Geography shows broad land/water and plot shapes plus ice barriers without duplicating Coast terrain. The political picture intentionally remains ownership-only, because physical symbols would either hide owned water or appear inconsistently only outside borders. Normally stable geography, terrain, and bonuses appear once at setup/load, with exact later exceptions preserved by coordinate-bearing map/bonus-change rows. When different bonuses share one resampled cell, `+` exposes the collision while exact setup rows retain their identities and coordinates. The pictures are omniscient diagnostic maps rather than a civilization's contemporary knowledge; notably, the bonus picture includes resources that no civilization has the technology to reveal yet. The initial feature picture shows how jungle, forest, flood plains, oases, and ice affect settling, movement, health, and yields; feature and political pictures then repeat at each full interval and victory snapshot to show forest/jungle clearing and regrowth, late-game fallout, territorial expansion, conquest, and collapse across the run. Blank water and dotted featureless land keep feature and bonus pictures readable without overlaying geography. Each layer also reports its logical-cell total, exact drawing-character total, and per-symbol cell counts, so broad composition and change can be measured without manually counting the picture; horizontal visual repetition does not inflate these cell counts. Separate structured rows directly record player pollution, its sources, city health, global warming, and exact bonuses; the maps supply spatial context. Clean fixed-width rows preserve the generated map's proportions in horizontal, square, or vertical maps; legends map configurable symbols and runtime numeric IDs to terrain/feature/bonus types, while political symbols map to player IDs whose setup rows provide the civilization and displayed player name. XML defines control the picture bounds, monospace-width correction, and validated symbols.
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6022+) Note: it currently contains spoilers: a spoiler-free implementation was considered, so the human/active player can give to an LLM or such their play summary and get LLM or such suggestions or feedback/analysis, but it would require an extensive rework, so currently this is not supported; therefore, `SASGameRecord` log should as of now not be treated as a spoiler-free player-advice export. Note 2: depending on `SAS_GAME_RECORD_INTERVAL_TURNS_UNSCALED_GAMESPEED` value, the last turn may not appear in the log: for example, an autoplay run of 200 turns ends at T200 and thus does not seem to show the T200 data, only up to T190, so autoplay at least 201 turns to show T200 for example if you have this issue. For stronger LLM analysis, also provide compact source context when possible; for example, the AdvCiv-SAS light source ZIP from [`make_light_source_zip.py`](/LLM_Helpers/README.md#make_light_source_zippy) includes useful source/XML/docs/helper context and full `SASGameRecord` examples while staying compact enough to upload and share. See also the full raw [SASGameRecord example log](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/SASGameRecord_log/SASGameRecord_example.log), and [README.md (DLL Logging)](/README.md#dll-logging).
 
